The four form widgets android renders as a text field, a dropdown and a bar,
pinned together: three are declared overrides in codegen/platform/android and
`select` alone is still a case in compose_ir.go, so converting it shows as a
diff here.

input and datepicker are both Compose's OutlinedTextField. The placeholder is
its `label`, which takes content rather than a value, so the intrinsic declares
it as a slot -- and the override's `if placeholder != ""` is the whole of that
slot's body, so a field given no placeholder passes no `label` argument at all.
That is not the same widget as one labelled with nothing: `label = {}` still
reserves the space and notches the outline. It only folds that way for a
placeholder the compiler can see; a run-time one keeps the argument and the
`if` inside it.

datepicker's `value` is an `option<date>`, which is what lets the override show
nothing for a date nobody picked -- a `date`'s zero is the first day of year
one and would otherwise pre-fill every empty field with it.

select is what is left, and it is left for its state: `expanded` is numbered
per rendered node, which an override has no way to ask for.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `value = plain.*label` -- `plain` is the field given no placeholder: it passes no label argument at all, not an empty one
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `!= ""` -- the override's placeholder test is a constant here and folds away; it does not reach the emitted lambda

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

window {
    var name = ""
    var fruit = ""
    var plain = ""
    var birthday option<date> = null

    input(placeholder="Name", value=name, @input(e) { name = e.value })
    input(value=plain)
    select(options=["Apple", "Banana"], placeholder="Pick", value=fruit)
    progress(value=2.0, max=10.0)
    datepicker(placeholder="Birthday")
    datepicker(value=birthday)
}
